  "articleBody": "You’ve got a new window, and you’re ready to install it, but wait — is that opening actually square, plumb, and level? It's a super common problem folks run into, and it can throw off the whole installation if not handled correctly. Often, it comes down to not really understanding the manufacturer's rough opening specs. People sometimes think the rough opening should be the exact size of the window, but that's a big no-no. You need a little extra clearance for shimming and sealing everything up tight. If you don't account for that, you might end up with an opening that's either too snug or too wide, which makes getting a proper, airtight seal nearly impossible. And trust me, you don't want drafts or water leaks down the line. It's also easy to measure in just one spot and assume the whole opening is good. But walls can be tricky, so measuring in multiple places is key to catching any inconsistencies early on. Getting those diagonals checked and making sure it's all perfectly square *before* you order your window or even start sheathing and flashing is crucial. If it's off, you'll be kicking yourself later! When you're not sure, especially if it's a load-bearing wall or you're dealing with complex header sizing, it's really smart to get a qualified framer or builder involved. They can help prevent costly mistakes and make sure your window opening is perfectly prepped for a smooth installation. Don't risk it; call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for expert help.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
